{
  "total_prepetition_claim": 125000,
  "admin_503b9": {
    "amount": 78100,
    "invoices": ["MS-7788", "MS-7815"]
  },
  "general_unsecured": 46900,
  "reclamation": {
    "available": false,
    "why": "Technically still exercisable but practically worthless, so not relied on. Under 11 U.S.C. Sec. 546(c)(1) (library: bc_503b9_546c.md), goods received within 45 days pre-petition (on/after 2026-01-24: MS-7740, MS-7788, MS-7815) may be reclaimed only by written demand not later than 45 days after receipt or 20 days after the petition (2026-03-30) where the 45 days expire post-commencement; no such written reclamation demand has been made (the 2026-03-12 letter was an automated payment-demand, not a demand for return of goods), and the window for the earliest goods (received 2026-02-04) closes imminently. Decisively, the library states reclamation is subject to prior liens - here the DIP lender's blanket lien - so reclamation would recover nothing. The same MS-7788/MS-7815 goods are already captured by the Sec. 503(b)(9) administrative claim, which is the effective route. Client gave no go/no-go and no facts on goods identifiability; default recommendation is NO-GO (no spend). Assumption: Mesa holds no purchase-money security interest."
  },
  "bar_date": "2026-06-11",
  "preference": {
    "transfers_in_90_days": 180000,
    "ordinary_course_defense": {
      "protected": 95000,
      "exposed": 85000,
      "reasoning": "Petition 2026-03-10; 90-day window opens 2025-12-10 (11 U.S.C. Sec. 547(b)(4), (f); library: bc_547.md). Four payments fall inside: 2025-12-18 $51,000 (45 days after invoice, ACH), 2026-01-22 $44,000 (43 days, ACH), 2026-02-10 $48,200 (29 days, ACH), 2026-02-27 $36,800 (25 days, WIRE demanded by Mesa). Protected (Sec. 547(c)(2)(A)): the 12/18 and 1/22 payments (=$95,000) match the parties' 12-month history of 43-47 day ACH payments on a regular cadence. Exposed (= $85,000): the 2/10 payment (29 days - well outside the 43-47 day history, arriving after Mesa's unilateral 2026-01-20 shortening of terms to NET 15, and the library states payments under shortened terms generally fall outside (c)(2)(A)) and the 2/27 partial payment (25 days, wire on demand - the library expressly names 'wire on demand' under 'pressure' as outside (c)(2)(A)). Sec. 547(c)(2)(B) (ordinary industry terms) could protect the 2/10 payment, but the client has NO industry payment-terms evidence, so this fallback is currently unsupported - flagged open. Burden on these defenses is Mesa's (Sec. 547(g))."
    },
    "new_value_defense": 85000,
    "net_exposure_estimate": 0
  },
  "stay_violations": [
    "2026-03-12: 'FINAL NOTICE - pay $210,000 within 5 days or we refer to collections' letter, mailed and emailed 2 days post-petition - an act to collect a pre-petition claim, 11 U.S.C. Sec. 362(a)(6) (library: bc_362.md: 'Dunning letters ... after the petition are acts to collect'). Client states this was generated by an AUTOMATED notice program triggered at 30 days late. Automation does not cure the violation, and the program is presumed STILL RUNNING against Redwood: every further automated letter/email is a fresh violation and the automation pattern is strong evidence of willfulness under Sec. 362(k) once Mesa knew of the case.",
    "2026-03-15: collection call to Redwood AP demanding payment, made after Redwood told the caller it had filed Chapter 11 - a collection call post-petition, Sec. 362(a)(6). Client (Frank) adds that 'Lori' called twice - the dunning log documents only the one 3/15 call, so at least one call is undocumented; dates/content unknown and must be pinned down for any Sec. 362(k) response. Being told of the filing on 3/15 fixes knowledge for everything after.",
    "2026-03-18: email to Redwood CFO threatening to 'hold all future shipments and report to credit bureaus' - sent after Mesa knew of the petition; the credit-bureau threat is expressly an act to collect per the library, Sec. 362(a)(6). Its post-knowledge timing makes willfulness (Sec. 362(k): actual damages, costs, attorneys' fees, possible punitive damages) very hard to contest."
  ],
  "immediate_actions": [
    "TODAY: suspend the automated dunning program for the Redwood account - remove/suppress the account from the 30-day notice cycle and confirm in writing that no further letters, emails, calls, or credit-bureau reporting are scheduled. Repeat automated notices post-knowledge are fresh Sec. 362(a)(6) violations and aggravate Sec. 362(k) exposure.",
    "TODAY: written stop-collection instruction to AR staff (Frank, Lori, credit dept); instruct all Redwood contact to route through counsel. Reconstruct and preserve the record: exact dates/content of both AP calls, all automated letters/emails sent since 2026-03-10, and the 3/18 email.",
    "Have counsel send a short corrective communication to debtor's counsel confirming Mesa will abide by the stay (caps the willfulness story; do not send any further demand letters from Mesa itself).",
    "RESPOND TO REDWOOD'S OVERTURE NOW: Redwood's counsel already asked whether Mesa will keep supplying on normal terms if the old invoices are paid - that is the critical-vendor bargain, and the vehicle is the pending motion at Dkt. 44 (hearing 2026-04-02). The CEO's 'pay first' reply is achievable ONLY through that motion: a Kmart-type order pays the pre-petition claim up front in exchange for continued supply at customary trade terms. Direct Mesa's CEO to contact debtor's counsel (through counsel) before April 2 and engage on the motion's terms.",
    "Prepare Official Form 410 proof of claim for $125,000 to be filed well before the 2026-06-11 bar date (Fed. R. Bankr. P. 3002(c), 3003(c); library: frbp_3002_3003.md) - late claims barred absent excusable neglect. Do NOT claim the automated letter's $210,000 figure; the records support $125,000.",
    "Assert the $78,100 Sec. 503(b)(9) administrative portion separately, by motion or the procedure the bar-date order specifies (library: frbp_3002_3003.md).",
    "Reclamation (Sec. 546(c)): calendar 2026-03-30 as the outside date for a written demand, but default is NO-GO - it is gutted by the DIP lender's blanket lien (library: bc_503b9_546c.md) and the client gave no go/no-go. Negotiating lever only.",
    "Quantify and reserve for preference exposure ($180,000 received in the window; realistic residual near $0 after defenses, see preference block); no industry terms data exists for Sec. 547(c)(2)(B) so the (c)(2)(A) history defense plus Sec. 547(c)(4) new value must carry the case; remember Sec. 502(d) - the court can disallow Mesa's claim until any preference liability is paid (library: bc_502d_setoff.md)."
  ]
}

